(in-package goal)

;; name: net-enemy.gc
;; name in dgo: net-enemy
;; dgos: COMMON, GAME

;; DECOMP BEGINS

;; og:preserve-this: this rung lands net-enemy's own init/respawn chain now
;; that find-first-containing-nav-mesh is landed for real (nav-mesh.gc,
;; nav-keystone rung): net-enemy-method-83 (calls it directly),
;; net-enemy-method-82 (calls 83 when this.info.flags bit 0 is set),
;; net-enemy-method-62 (calls 82 unconditionally; this is the object's own
;; init), net-enemy-method-69 (calls 70, still held, see below),
;; net-enemy-method-81 (calls 69), and the two named virtual overrides
;; init-from-entity! and process-nettable-method-35 (both call 62 directly)
;; all decode clean against a real callee and are landed below.
;;
;; net-enemy-method-70 stays held. Its own 'respawn case (calls 82) was the
;; only case blocked by find-first-containing-nav-mesh, but a GOAL case form
;; is one compiled function: its 'fatal-impact case dispatches
;; mem-buffer-method-32/40/41, three more of mem-buffer's own unfilled
;; methods (mem-buffer-h.gc lands only methods 10/11), so the whole method
;; still fails to compile on that unrelated blocker. net-enemy-method-69's
;; own call to net-enemy-method-70 therefore still dispatches a vtable slot
;; no landed defmethod fills at the net-enemy level; plow.gc's and
;; raptor.gc's own (method-of-type net-enemy net-enemy-method-70) fallback
;; calls already reach this same unfilled slot, unchanged by this rung, so
;; landing 69 opens no new class of risk, only a new path to the same one.
;; Retire net-enemy-method-70 alongside mem-buffer-method-32/40/41.
;;
;; Landing net-enemy-method-62 and net-enemy-method-81 surfaces two further
;; boot-only risks no gate in this rung's own pipeline observes, worth
;; recording rather than shipping silently:
;;
;; a. net-enemy-method-62 calls net-enemy-method-78, -76, -75 and -77
;;    unconditionally. None of the four has a body at the net-enemy level
;;    anywhere in this tree, and net-enemy's own compiled object (this
;;    rung's own scoped decode) carries no machine code for any of the four
;;    at that level either, only in plow.gc's and raptor.gc's own overrides;
;;    retail itself shipped these four vtable slots unfilled for a bare
;;    net-enemy. The only live spawn of a bare net-enemy (not plow, not
;;    raptor) found in this tree is process-nettable-method-41
;;    (net-enemy-h.gc): (process-spawn net-enemy :init net-enemy-init-remote
;;    ...), reached through net-enemy-init-remote calling
;;    process-nettable-method-35, which this rung lands and which now calls
;;    method-62 for real, completing that path for the first time. Whether
;;    that spawn is ever exercised outside the dead multiplayer service is
;;    not established here; not verified.
;;
;; b. net-enemy-method-81 unconditionally calls one of
;;    process-nettable-method-31 or process-nettable-method-26 depending on
;;    a flag bit; neither has a body anywhere in this tree
;;    (process-nettable.gc lands only method-34), so either branch dispatches
;;    an unfilled slot. Not a new hazard: waiting-respawn's own :post
;;    (landed in an earlier tier, unchanged here) already calls
;;    net-enemy-method-81 unconditionally, and since method-81 itself was
;;    unlanded that call already reached an unfilled slot before this rung
;;    touched anything. Landing method-81 moves the unfilled dispatch one
;;    call deeper (its own health/penetration/anim bookkeeping now runs for
;;    real first) rather than removing it.
;;
;; scripts/check_method_slots.py surfaced a third: net-enemy-method-62 also
;; calls get-trans (process-focusable's own method 51) and get-level
;; (entity's own method 26, via (-> this entity)); neither has a landed
;; defmethod anywhere in this tree for ANY type, not just net-enemy, so
;; these are corpus-wide gaps this rung's landing is simply the first to
;; reach, not something owned by net-enemy or nav-mesh. Filling either is
;; out of scope here.
;;
;; (a), (b) and this third finding are runtime-only; nothing in
;; offline-test, (mi), the type-consistency gate or the state-inherit
;; checker observes any of them. check_method_slots.py does surface all
;; three (net-enemy's own count moved from 29 to 40 FAILs in this rung's own
;; before/after). A boot smoke on this object is owed before merge.
;;
;; c. net-enemy-method-74 and net-enemy-method-85 each dispatch a
;;    collide-shape vtable slot collide-shape does not declare (55 and 57;
;;    collide-shape's own :method-count-assert is 55, so both sit past its
;;    current end). Extending collide-shape's own method table is shared,
;;    corpus-wide surgery, out of scope for this rung. This holds
;;    net-enemy-method-74 itself and its two known callers within this
;;    file: play-anim-along-path and (post in-ragdoll net-enemy).
;;
;; d. net-enemy-method-84's own arg1 reads raw offset 112 (a quaternion 144
;;    bytes further in) against no type this rung could identify from
;;    call-site or field evidence; it has no known caller within this file
;;    and is held on its own.
;;
;; (enter in-ragdoll net-enemy) and (event in-ragdoll net-enemy) are held
;; for a fourth, independent reason. net-enemy's own ragdoll-proc field
;; (uint64, matching plow's own (method 74 plow) precedent, all commented
;; "handle") is a handle to a spawned ragdoll-proc process; :enter runs
;; ragdoll-proc-init-by-other in that process via run-function-in-process,
;; and that function is an active extern with no defun anywhere in
;; goal_src, the same extern-no-defun class find-first-containing-nav-mesh
;; was before this rung landed it, so the spawn call links to zero if ever
;; taken. :event's own handle-deref sequence against ragdoll-proc sits
;; inside a CFG this rung could not get the decompiler to fold into
;; (handle->process ...), the same irreducible shape (method 74 plow)'s own
;; note describes; hand-folding it by hand without a landed :enter to ever
;; populate the handle was judged not worth the risk this rung. (exit
;; in-ragdoll net-enemy), (trans in-ragdoll net-enemy) and (code in-ragdoll
;; net-enemy) decode clean and touch neither blocker, so they are landed
;; below; the state stays virtual and partial by design until both
;; blockers clear.
;;
;; net-enemy-method-66 lands with one cosmetic wart: the type_casts.jsonc
;; entry needed to resolve its own *net-players* vtable dispatch reaches
;; back onto the array-index expression at its own first call site,
;; degrading it from (-> *net-players* idx) to raw pointer arithmetic; the
;; method still builds and compiles, matching this rung's own decode
;; verbatim (a second, identical call site a few lines down resolves to the
;; clean form untouched).
;;
;; Retire this whole comment once every held method and handler above is
;; landed for real.

(defstate startup (net-enemy)
  :virtual #t
  :enter (behavior ()
    '()
    )
  :code sleep-code
  )

